### S2 clang's -verify tests — started (62ea29a, TODO T20)
`bench/vcc-verify.py -j 6 [-v] [PAT]`.  Each `%clang_cc1 … -verify` line in
`test/{C,Sema,Preprocessor,Lexer,Parser}` is a test.  The expected
diagnostics are in the file (`// expected-error {{...}}`).  `test/C` is
organized by standard, WG14 paper and Defect Report (`drs/`), and is what
comes closest to test262.  The harness reads the directives from raw source
with `#line`; skipped `#if` branches it finds out via clang.  The matching
checks the diagnostic's level, file, line and count.  Vcc's wording may
differ from clang's; our own snapshots verify vcc's full messages.
`--match-messages` is an optional comparison with clang's text.  Clang's side
is cached and its original contract is still checked.  Separate
`results.locations.json` and `results.messages.json` show changes within the
same comparison policy.

**Linux x86-64 2026-11-11:** `bench/vcc-verify.py --target
x86_64-linux-gnu -j 6` now gives **443/1640 passed, 1197 failures and no
crashes** with independent message text.  The ARM target's front-end check
gives **450/1805 passed, 1355 failures, no crashes**; the test sources are
not run as programs in this suite.  This frees 34 tests per target from pure
text requirements, without any semantic compiler change.  The previous strict
text comparison gave 409 and 416 passed respectively.  Results and references
are kept apart per target; arm64 is selected with `--target
aarch64-linux-gnu`.  The remaining failures require investigation of
semantics, diagnostic level, line, count and recovery.

**Status 2026-11-10:** 412/1841 valid tests passed (22 %), 1 crash.

