Full local Wasm run after the CI fixes: **1809 passed**, 31 explicit XFAIL
(20 newly covered plus 11 earlier), 141 filtered, no unexpected failures or
XPASS.  TSan's daemon stress also revealed that `gen_call` was inlined into
recursive ARM expressions: its 8 KiB argument buffer was reserved for every
arithmetic node.  A separate function boundary reduces the AArch64 TSan
frame in `gen_inner` from 8608 to 432 bytes.  The unchanged 250-level test
and the subsequent valid TU now pass with the same 2 MiB worker stack; stack
limits, tests and sanitizer checks have not been changed.

### S4 gcc.dg — started (2026-11-11)
**Combined semantic follow-up milestone at `74f6ec4`:** C90, nested
GNU range initializations, declaration lifetime after labels and wide
rotations have been validated together.  Four affected unchanged GCC cases
pass on both Linux targets and both builds; five focused runtime tests also
pass at O2/O3.  Full gates, explicit reference exceptions and Wasm/ASan
results are in PLAN-release.md and `work/results/semantic-stack-74/`.  No
full S4 pass sweep is claimed.

**C90 digraphs and statement scope (2026-11-11):** the unchanged
`c90-digraph-1.c` and `c90-scope-1.c` now go from runtime abort to passing
on Linux x86-64 and arm64, both unity and nonunity.  Strict C89/C90 does not
read digraphs; C94 and GNU89 do.  Selection statements/loops get their own
implicit scopes only in C99, while explicit blocks always keep their scope.
`bench/vcc-c90.py` checks seven modes against clang, token pasting,
stdin/-D and 21 mixed header-cache jobs per target/build.  Full PP parity
is 920/920 in both cmp and batch before and after the lexer change; sema run
+ batch is 887/887 afterwards.  No upstream flag has been removed.

`bench/vcc-gcc-dg.py -j 6 --target x86_64-linux-gnu` runs direct
`gcc.dg/*.c` with `dg-do run` (1276 files) and direct
`gcc.dg/torture/*.c` (2208 files).  `--target aarch64-linux-gnu` uses
QEMU and a Linux sysroot.  The harness reads target filters, options and
companion files; clang is oracle and filter.  The root's default flags
follow `dg.exp` (`-ansi -pedantic-errors`, replaced by `dg-options`).
Torture runs at -O0 by default; `--matrix` selects the six non-LTO
configurations of `gcc-dg.exp`.  Unknown directives/selectors are skipped
explicitly; successful exit follows `dg-do run`, while not yet supported
`dg-output`/`dg-final` are not counted.

**Bounded baseline:** `--suite torture --limit 400 '^torture/pr'`
gives x86 270 passed/11 failures/119 filtered and arm64 273/8/119,
no compiler crashes.  This is 400 of 1563 matching PR files at -O0,
not the whole suite.  A runtime segfault that was found (`pr116454-1`) is
fixed: returning a scalar to `_Complex` is now converted before code
generation.  Both upstream cases `pr116454-{1,2}` and a regression with
scalar and complex return conversions pass on both targets.

`--suite errors` now compares all error lines against clang for the 1214
direct `gcc.dg/*.c` that have `dg-error`.  Relative lines, `dg-line` and
target selectors are supported; each missing or extra error line is a
failure.  Clang errors outside active annotations and unknown contracts are
filtered explicitly.  This measures error-line parity, not GCC's message text
or warnings/notes.  Baseline: x86 359 passed/369 failures/1 crash/482
filtered/3 reference failures; arm64 353/373/0/485/3.  The crash
(`cwsc0.c`) came from an incomplete `void` argument in an unprototyped call
and is fixed with its own regression.  The targeted check no longer
crashes, but the static-chain builtin is still missing and the upstream case
is therefore not green.  Full reruns after the fix remain.
The array type check is now at the declarator itself, also for fields,
typedefs and parameters: `array-9.c` and `array-15.c` pass in targeted
checks on both Linux targets.  The regression checks both invalid element
types and valid incomplete outer dimensions/VLA prototypes.
Subscripting of function pointers and pointers to incomplete types is also
rejected now: `array-8.c` and `pointer-arith-{1,2,3}.c` pass on both
targets.  The isolated package's array/incomplete/subscript diagnostic tests
pass 10/10, both with a unity-built and a file-by-file-built compiler.

**Extended frozen baseline `5e17f47`:** all 1563 matching
`torture/pr` files at -O0 have now been checked, without `--limit`.
X86: **1189 passed, 18 failures, 356 filtered, no compiler crashes
or reference crashes**.  ARM/QEMU: **1184 passed, 6 failures, 373
filtered, no crashes**.  This is still a subset of S4,
not the whole gcc.dg or the optimization matrix.  The x86 failures include
interrupt ABI, inline asm, missing builtins and a reproduced failure in
128-bit shifts (`pr69909`); ARM has, among others, the same front-end gaps
and one assembly failure.  The `pow`/`fabsl` link failures are real
differences in lowering/optimization, not reasons to add `-lm` to the rig
when upstream does not ask for it.  Result:
`work/results/vroom-milestone-s4-pr-{x86,arm}.json`.

After the baseline two front-end gaps are fixed: `__builtin_c23_va_start`
(also ellipsis without a named parameter and an ignored optional second
operand) and generic `__builtin_stdc_rotate_left/right` with type-preserving
modulo rotation and single evaluation.  `pr48731` and `pr117492-1` compile
and assemble on both Linux targets.  Two runtime regressions per target and
invalid operand types are checked; unity/nonunity and the kernel's sema
887/887 in both modes pass.  This is targeted evidence; S4's full baseline
numbers above have not been recomputed after the fix.

Same bit width for a signed scalar to an unsigned vector lane is now accepted
as in clang: `pr103181` and `pr127379` compile and assemble on both Linux
targets; the first also runs and matches clang.  Regressions keep the
rejection of unsigned to signed and wider scalars.  Unity/nonunity,
positive/negative tests and kernel sema 887/887 in run/batch pass.  This is
a separate front-end fix after the CI milestone's frozen tree; PR #28's
green CI applies to its own tree and does not verify this later change.

**Full direct S4 baseline, frozen `aa584e2` (2026-11-11):** now all
**3484 files per target** are run, 1276 direct `dg-do run` and 2208 direct
torture files at -O0, without `--limit` or a PR name filter.  X86:
**1803 passed, 83 failures, 1596 filtered, 2 reference failures**.  ARM/QEMU:
**1783 passed, 74 failures, 1625 filtered, 2 reference failures**.  No
compiler crash.  The x86 failures are 36 runtime, 29 compile and 18
link failures; ARM has 31 runtime, 24 compile, 17 link and 2
assembly failures.  Both targets have a clang timeout in `float-exact-1.c`
and a clang SIGSEGV in `vla-tert-1.c`.  The failure count is test cases, not
unique bugs.  The 83 x86 failures are grouped into 32 signatures, among
others VLA side effects, wide bit-fields, interrupt ABI, intrinsics support
and optimization contracts.  `fwrapv-2.c` and two tag-alias tests require a
separate UB/optimization analysis; their aborts are not in themselves
evidence of miscompilation of well-defined C.  Reports:
`work/results/s4-all-{x86,arm}.json`, `s4-all-summary.json` and
`s4-all-classification.txt`.  Compiler SHA-256:
`21aba03551f35a89d577a36a268051fee25dc757f296161981261a1d2b91bdbb`.
The earlier PR subset does not replace this full baseline.  The whole
optimization matrix and nested suites still remain.

The same frozen compiler's own runtime regressions pass **135/135 x86**
(five explicit reference/target exceptions) and **140/140 ARM** (no
exceptions).  C23 va_start and signed vector scalars also pass on Wasm;
the rotation test's `_BitInt` part hits a verified older Wasm gap and
must be kept visible at the next premerge.  The three `pr84682` asm cases
are now green in a targeted run on both Linux targets without new code
changes; asm runtime passes 7/7 x86 with two earlier exceptions and 9/9 ARM.

128-bit bit-fields are fixed on both Linux targets: ARM's invalid
`UBFM #64` and x86's raw object read are replaced by word-wise extraction
and insertion with signed extension.  `pr89091` passes in five GCC
configurations per target (one explicit upstream filtering).  Regressions
cover 63-128 bit fields, packed offsets, high words, truncated assignment
results, increments and neighbouring fields, including objects against a
guard page.  Unity/nonunity and targeted runtime tests at -O0/-O2 pass on
both targets.  The full baseline numbers above still apply before this fix.

VLA `sizeof` no longer loses the operand's side effects: all three
emitter paths evaluate the array address operand, and the prepass visits it
and the VLA bounds so that constant local optimization does not use old
values.  `typename-vla-{1,2}` and `vla-stexp-9` now pass on both Linux
targets.  The regression covers comma, pointer increment and statement
expressions, including that a fixed `sizeof` remains unevaluated.
Unity/nonunity and -O0/-O2 run tests pass; six other cases in the VLA
cluster remain.

`vla-24` is also fixed: explicit `alloca` moves the saved stack pointers of
already active VLA scopes, so function-lifetime storage survives the end of
the scope.  Later VLA scopes still reclaim storage, verified with 16384
iterations after an older alloca (no general disabling of stack reclaim).
A frozen combination of `5e6f247` and the fix builds in unity/nonunity; the
whole own runtime suite gives **143/143 x86** (five existing reference
exceptions) and **148/148 ARM/QEMU**, without cache.  Four targeted VLA
regressions per target pass at -O0 and in the nonunity build; the original
GCC test passes 1/1 per target.  Result:
`s4-vla-lifetime-integrated-{x86,arm}.json`.  Wasm is checked separately for
assembly, not runtime-verified here.  Side effects of the remaining VLA
bounds and inline alloca are not fixed.

With full `-ffast-math`, a correctly identified external
`pow(double, 0.25)` is now lowered to two hardware square roots on Linux
x86/ARM.  Local/asm-named functions, disabled builtins and stricter FP flags
keep their calls; `-mgeneral-regs-only` rejects the operation before any FP
output is written.  `pr41094` passes in the x86 harness; the ARM oracle at
-O0 itself requires libm and is filtered, while a separate -O2 oracle and
both vcc output paths run the original test without libm.  Unity/nonunity,
four runtime regressions per target, 84 flag checks, 28 strict IEEE tests,
24 no-FP checks and separation in both declaration cache paths pass.
Existing driver/predefine failures are exactly unchanged against the base
(137/212, the same 75 failures); twelve targeted macro/order tests pass.

Full rerun at `ad4cf9a`, all 3484 configurations per target:
**x86 1811 passed, 75 failures, 1596 filtered, two oracle failures**;
**ARM 1792 passed, 65 failures, 1625 filtered, two oracle failures**.
That is eight and nine fixed cases respectively from the frozen baseline, no
earlier passing case regresses, no compiler crashes and unchanged
filterings/oracle failures.  Full comparison: `work/results/s4-integrated-summary.json`.
The numbers apply to the direct selection at the default configuration, not
the whole optimization matrix or nested sub-suites.

Static initialization of wide bit-fields now keeps all 128 constant bits
before the field width's truncation, without undefined shifts of a 64-bit
value.  The remaining `pr89037` thereby passes on both targets; three other
bit-field cases were already green.  The new regression covers signed 66-bit
fields, packed 119-bit fields, 128-bit fields and neighbouring bits.  Four
GCC tests and six targeted runtime tests pass per target, unity/nonunity and
the new regression at -O0.

File-scope C23 `constexpr` objects get internal linkage also after an
earlier `extern` declaration.  `c23-constexpr-2a` now links and runs on both
Linux targets.  A new two-file test checks separate scalar, array and
struct objects with the same name in all four clang/vcc combinations,
unity/nonunity on x86 and ARM/QEMU.  The ABI rig supports the existing
`vcc-flags` format per source file for the language version.  Kernel sema is
887/887 in both per-process and batch mode.  Other storage class diagnostics
are not changed by this link fix.

`__builtin_powi/powif/powil` use the compiler runtime symbols
`__powidf2`, `__powisf2` and the target's `__powixf2`/`__powitf2`
respectively.  This stops recursion into the user's own wrapper functions in
`pr19402-2`, without changing ordinary function calls.  An isolated fix
against `ad4cf9a` passes the original test and regression on both Linux
targets, unity/nonunity; the regression covers the helper ABI, one evaluation
of each operand, negative exponents, INT_MIN, signed zero and NaN/inf raised
to zero.  Kernel sema 887/887 in both modes.  The combined full totals above
apply before this fix and the following integration is verified separately.

Scalar float/double `fabs` and `copysign`, both explicit builtin and a
correctly identified library declaration, are lowered to integer masks.
This preserves signaling NaN bits and signed zero without unnecessary libm
calls.  Each operand is evaluated once; local/asm-renamed or disabled
library functions remain calls.  The four GCC cases
`builtins-{48,49,50}` and `pr56125` pass on both Linux targets in both
build modes.  A bitwise regression, flag/name tests and kernel sema
887/887 in both modes are verified on an isolated fix against `ad4cf9a`.
Integration with the upcoming x86 function-specific target policy requires
a separate effective-feature check; this is not claimed fully verified here.

VLA bounds in new cast/compound-literal/`va_arg` type names are now
evaluated and saved where the type is created.  Earlier typedef bounds are
reused; `__auto_type` lets the initializer drive the order without double
evaluation.  The prepass sees the declaration bounds and replay moves the VLA
type operand as a type, not an integer.  `vla-{12,13,14}` and `vla-stexp-7`
thereby pass on both Linux targets (the latter crashed earlier on ARM).  The
isolated fix passes unity/nonunity, -O0/-O2 regressions and kernel sema
887/887 in both modes.  The new regression also runs green in wasm64 via
emsdk/node.  Two `typeof` cases, `pr114831-2` and `vla-15`, and inline alloca
remain.

The following `typeof` fix evaluates the VM operand once and saves new
dimensions from the original cached dimensions, even if the operand changes
the bound variables.  Thereby `pr114831-2` and `vla-15` also pass: six
targeted upstream tests per Linux target, no exceptions.  A new
multi-dimensional regression passes with clang/vcc at -O0/-O2 in both build
modes, and actual wasm64 runtime (two VLA tests).  Kernel sema is 887/887 in
both modes.  A separate remaining failure: a later variable declaration with
a VM typedef can still re-evaluate its already saved bound; general typedef
reuse and inline alloca are not done.

The following emitter fix now reuses the VM typedef's saved bounds also at
later variable declarations.  The cache distinguishes function and inline
instance; the same declaration's code still runs on each loop iteration.  A
new regression checks changed bound variables, nested typedefs, nineteen
loop iterations and two inline instances.  Seven relevant runtime tests per
Linux target/build mode, -O0/-O2 and three actual wasm64 tests pass.  A
larger VLA selection still has one failure (`vla-16`, compound-literal
lvalue), ten unchanged reference/contract filterings and Clang's existing
front-end crash in `vla-tert-1`; these are not hidden.

VM compound literals now keep the lvalue category through an internal
address sequence, and prefix increment can parse compound-literal operands.
Wasm reserves the dimension cache entry before recursive bound emission.
Thereby `vla-16` also passes: the whole direct VLA selection's clang-valid
configurations are **11/11 per Linux target**, ten unchanged explicit
reference/contract filterings and one Clang crash are reported separately.
Eight own VLA tests per target/build mode, -O0/-O2, four actual wasm64
tests, kernel sema 887/887 in both modes and diagnostics 217/217 pass.

`__builtin_complex` constructs complex numbers without losing signed zero,
NaN bits or argument side effects.  Complex `++`/`--` update only the real
part with one read, one computation and one write; the imaginary part is
preserved.  C2y mode is separated from C23 also in the replay cache's key,
and case ranges are accepted correctly in C2y.  Four original complex tests
and new regressions pass on both Linux targets, unity/nonunity; kernel sema
887/887 in both modes.  Sixteen mixed C23/C2y cache batches check both warm
orders and the cache paths.  Atomic complex updates are still rejected
explicitly and tracked separately in TODO.md.

x87 to unsigned64 now uses both value halves around 2^63 instead of
only signed `fistp`.  The regression checks boundaries, truncation, one
operand evaluation and restored x87 control word.  The original
`fp-int-convert-long-double` and the new regression pass on both Linux
targets in unity/nonunity; ARM's existing binary128 path is kept separate.

x87 to/from 128-bit integers use XF runtime helpers with the ordinary
call classifier (stack/x87 and integer registers respectively), not TF's
binary128 ABI.  A new regression covers signed/unsigned boundaries,
truncation and one operand evaluation on both Linux targets and build modes.
The corresponding unchanged GCC timode tests now pass on x86.

ARM's constant integer to float is rounded directly to float and does not
lose the sticky bit through an intermediate double.  Integer to binary128
uses the existing nearest-even helper instead of truncation.  New 64/128-bit
boundary tests pass on both targets/build modes.  The unchanged applicable
GCC conversion matrix is now **35 passing x86 configurations** and **30 ARM
configurations**, zero failures; existing upstream/oracle filterings are
unchanged.

The Wasm precheck keeps generic rotation tests mandatory and puts the
BitInt sub-tests in a separate runnable regression.  Constant
`fabs`/`copysign` are folded target-independently; the powi test compares negative infinity
directly instead of requiring a separate, still unsupported long-double
builtin.  These three full regressions run green in wasm64 and on both Linux
targets.  Two wide bit-field tests and a separate BitInt rotation are
explicit Wasm XFAILs that are still run, with TODO references: the same
sources already fail with the byte-verified upstream base `2339f69`.  The
Linux tests remain mandatory.  The Wasm failures are not hidden as passed or
skipped tests.

TSan found a real race when parallel emitter threads evaluated constant
initializers: a shared recursion chain pointed into other threads' stacks,
and implicit zero/string elements created new AST nodes during emission.
The recursion chain is now thread-local and keyed per frontend/c/symbol;
allocation-free subobject descriptions replace the AST mutations.  The
original reproducer goes from TSan exit66 to green with six threads.
Normal/nonunity/TSan pass three constexpr runtime tests on both Linux
targets, cycle diagnostics and kernel sema 887/887 in both modes.  A new
permanent 500-global regression uses the rig's `vcc-threads-same` marker for
repeated parallel emissions also in TSan CI.

A separate TSan race in the prepass's write/constant metadata is also
fixed: function-local facts are owned by the respective lazy walk; global
facts are initialized conservatively before the worker threads.  The emitter
threads remain parallel, and local constant propagation is kept.  **8036/8036**
O0/O2 outputs on both Linux targets are byte-identical against `6cda644`; the
real kernel object `map_kernel.o` is unchanged.  A new 200-function
regression with shared global writes passes in normal/nonunity/TSan on both
targets, the six-thread reproducer is clean and the permanent test requires
repeated threaded emission.

Full rerun on frozen `573f23b`, 3484 configurations per target:
**x86 1831 passed, 55 failures, 1596 filtered, two oracle failures**;
**ARM 1812 passed, 45 failures, 1625 filtered, two oracle failures**.
Twenty earlier failures per target are fixed compared with `ad4cf9a`, no
earlier passing case regresses and filterings/oracle failures are unchanged.
Unity/nonunity pass own applicable runtime tests (155 x86, 160 ARM),
diagnostics 217/217, sixteen cache batches and kernel sema 887/887 run/batch.
A cold Clang reference for `branch_far` timed out; the same source's kept,
byte-verified reference verifies its separate build mode.  Artifacts:
`work/results/candidate-573f23b-validation.json`, `s4-573f23b-summary.json`.
The numbers apply before the last two VLA follow-up fixes and the prepass fix.
Final code `0757803` is also verified with 15/15 affected runtime tests
per Linux target, diagnostics 217/217, the actual wasm64 suite 126/126 plus
27 documented XFAIL/10 reference exceptions, and two repeated TSan
threading regressions.  The whole test plan still remains to be completed.

**CI follow-up fixes, frozen `b97022e` (2026-11-11):** the sanitizer run
revealed signed host overflow when valid unsigned128 arithmetic was
evaluated in the front end and ARM emitter.  Addition, subtraction and
negation now use unsigned modularity before the target type's truncation.  A
separate regression in `20020720-1` came from the fabs sign-mask lowering
losing the result's non-negative fact; the fact is now kept without changing
the arguments' side effects or NaN behavior.  Combined Linux run passes
**160/160 own x86 tests** (five existing reference/target exceptions) and
**1882/1882 ARM tests**: 1612 GCC, 105 TCC and 165 own, with 127 unchanged
external filterings.  Each fix passes unity/nonunity and kernel sema
**887/887** individually and in batch; the unsigned fix also ASan sema.
Combined ASan/UBSan run passes **165/165 own ARM tests**, without
filterings.  The test rig now always writes the name of each failing case.
These numbers apply to the vendored run suite, not the larger external S4
corpus above.

Combined frozen checkpoint `7afcdea`, before the sign-mask/type-name fixes,
is verified in both build modes: **145/145 x86** (five reference exceptions),
**150/150 ARM/QEMU**, two-file constexpr ABI and three original GCC cases
per target.  Full binary hashes/logs: `work/results/checkpoint-7afcdea-validation.json`.

The runtime rigs now separate the reference compiler's signal/timeout/tool
failure from ordinary source rejections.  Oracle failures give a failure
status, are included in the summary and are not cached as permanent
exceptions.  Old misleading cache entries are invalidated.  The large
`branch_far` has an explicit limited reference time of 300 seconds; other
tests keep 60.  Fourteen offline groups inject failures into all three rigs,
and a full CLI injection gives 0 passed, 0 skipped, one oracle failure and
exit1.  Real `branch_far` and `signaling_nan` pass 2/2 on x86 and ARM/QEMU
with the new classification.

The CI follow-up check restored braced `dg-options`, among others the
`-fwrapv` that `930529-1` requires; the same Wasm reference goes from a 20 s
timeout to exit0 in 0.020 s.  The ARM guest's exception vector now gives
exit125 with diagnostics on a real guest failure, while an infinite loop is
still an oracle failure.  The native reference program's signal is reported
as a rejected runtime contract with signal and stderr; compiler/linker/QEMU/
Node failures and timeouts remain oracle failures.  Missing
`freopen`/`fscanf` prototypes in the rig's stdio header were fixed: two
failing native references now pass.  Full GCC run: **1615/1615 x86**, 86
explicit exceptions; ARM's complete vendored run suite **1883/1883**, 126
exceptions.  Wasm64 passes **1825/1825**, 34 XFAIL and 150 exceptions; 19 of
these follow the suite's explicit `label_values` requirement that Wasm
lacks.  No test name is used for that filter, and identical backend failures
without the directive remain failures.  All unexpected Wasm outcomes now
write the test name even without verbose mode.

After merging `main` (`9d022c3`) the same rig passes `62390af`
**1895/1895 ARM** (177 own tests, 126 explicit exceptions) and
**1839/1839 Wasm64** (140 own tests, 34 run XFAIL, 148 exceptions).
No unexpected failure, XPASS or oracle failure is reported.  The 35
checkout groups as well as real ARM exception probes and native stdio
also pass.  Logs: `work/results/pr37-merged-{arm,wasm}.log`.

Remaining:
- All remaining matching files and optimization configurations; nested
  directories with their own `.exp` drivers and LTO have no counterpart yet.
- More verified effective targets and conditional directives; unknown
  contracts must not become silent passes.
- The nested `dg-error` cases and remaining unsupported diagnostic contracts
  (among others source line mapping, line 0 and multiline directives).

**Done when:** the run tests as green as clang's; no file that clang accepts
is rejected by vcc.

