`work/results/kefir-float-final-{x86,arm}/`.

**Combined native milestone on top of the stack tip `1c5b5d1` (2026-11-11):**
Seven separate fixes combine x86 array object alignment, stepwise
binding of parked ARM inline arguments, atomic lock-free queries,
memory alternatives for scalar FP asm inputs, correct ELF storage of wide
strings with embedded zeros, `_ExtInt` alias and inlined alloca lifetime.
Explicit variable alignment and type alignment are preserved; dynamic Linux
atomics queries use libatomic and evaluate unresolved arguments once.
Statically decided queries do not evaluate the pointer expression.  Alloca
restores the callee stack lifetime after cleanup/return value without
erasing the arguments' or the caller's live allocations.

Final combined unity and file-by-file build: own tests x86 232/232 with
six existing reference exceptions; ARM 237/237 with one existing xfail.
ABI 33/33, hosted atomics 3/3 and seven focused regressions pass on
both targets and builds.  PP cmp 920/920 before the alias change; after all
changes cmp and batch 920/920.  Sema run/batch 887/887 on both builds,
diagnostics 225/225.  Independent alloca edge cases pass 18/18 (Clang/unity/
file-by-file x O0/O2/O3 x Linux x86/ARM).  GCC's unchanged `pr113596` with its
`-O2` directives passes on both targets and builds; all of S4 was not rerun.

Six unchanged Kefir cases (`atomic_builtins7`, `int128_function_call3`,
`pointer_subscr_const1`, `wide_string_literals1`, `extint1`,
`inline_assembly_fp3`) pass all four compiler combinations on x86
for both builds.  ARM passes the first three; the wide string case is rejected
by Clang because of upstream's signed-wchar typedef, `extint1` is rejected
because of the 310-bit reference type.  The FP asm case has an x86 guard and
does not count as ARM coverage.  A separate ExtInt oracle passes 24 combinations on x86
and 20 on ARM; ARM310 is checked against Python bytes in a further
bridge case.  No new full 875-case baseline is claimed.  Reproducible
commands, exact source/binary hashes and symlinks are in
`work/native-stack64/{run-gates.py,manifest.json,final-evidence.json,logs/}`.

**Combined Linux stack, frozen `a41361e` on top of `1c5b5d1`:**
all of Kefir's 875 runtime pairs are run first on x86, then ARM/QEMU,
with six workers, 30 s compile and 5 s run.  X86: **585 passed,
0 vcc compile failures, 6 run failures/timeouts**, 161 reference compile
failures, 6 reference link failures, 51 reference signal/timeout and 66
unsupported.  ARM: **461 passed, 0 vcc compile failures, 4 run failures**,
249/6/51 reference failures in the same categories and 104 unsupported.
Both runs end with status 1; neither exceptions nor remaining failures count
as passes.

The fixes cover atomic lock-free queries, ARM's parked inline
arguments, x86 array alignment, allowed memory operands for FP asm,
embedded zeros in wide strings, legacy `_ExtInt` and the lifetime of
inline `alloca` allocations.  The last also fixes GCC's `pr113596.c` on both
targets.  Both builds pass own Linux tests **232 x86 / 237 ARM**, ABI 33 per
target, PP parity 920, sema 887 in both modes and diagnostics 225.  ASan/UBSan
passes the same own Linux tests.  Wasm64: 189 passed, 38 run known XFAIL and
eleven reference exceptions.  The new `_ExtInt` test's XFAIL follows the
existing BitInt ABI gap: a corresponding `_BitInt` version is rejected
identically by the parent compiler on Wasm32 and Wasm64; no new native
failure is hidden.

Remaining x86 cases have grounds for unspecified padding/incompatible
function signatures, undefined shifts, an invalid asm contract,
`__builtin_unreachable` and a time limit.  ARM's `std_attributes18` has a
remaining actual runtime failure under investigation.  Other ARM cases
require separate classification of negative float-to-unsigned conversions
and pointers after object lifetime; they remain failures.  All of
S5b/G1/G2 are open.

Evidence: `work/results/kefir-native-stack-{x86,arm}/`,
`work/native-stack64/{manifest,final-evidence}.json` and
`work/results/native-stack64-ci-preflight/`.  Frozen compiler SHA256:
`9eeab468752c45bfec75b211fc822eddaa926343d498b35d20c3114cc86b64cf`.

### S6 ABI generators — rig done, failures being fixed
`gcc.dg/compat/struct-layout-1_generate.c` and clang's `utils/ABITest`
generate thousands of calls between two compilers.  Connected into
`bench/vcc-abi.py`: clang/vcc x caller/callee, Linux under qemu and
Apple natively.

`bench/vcc-abi.py --generated --target x86_64|aarch64` runs all four
combinations of clang/vcc as caller/callee.  Sources, commands and
output are saved.  Baseline with 1000 cases per generator: x86 GCC 654 passed,
122 failures, 1 vcc crash, 222 clang-filtered and 1 clang crash; LLVM
857/143.  ARM GCC 673/105/222 filtered and LLVM 987/13.
Fixes of vector calls, packed return values, union initialization,
field alignment and homogeneous ARM aggregates are now verified against the same cases.
The numbers are baselines, not a claim of final green runs.

New isolated run of the committed compiler `1009175`: x86 GCC
**777 passed, 0 vcc failures, 222 clang-filtered and 1 clang crash**;
LLVM **1000/1000**.  ARM GCC **769 passed, 9 failures, 222 filtered**;
LLVM **1000/1000**.  The nine ARM cases are verified against not yet
committed fixes for empty aggregates/HFA and varargs.  That the combined
working copy went green earlier does not replace isolated verification of
committed changes.  Sources/commands are in
`work/abi-generated/committed-{x86,arm}/`.

After the isolated fix of empty HFA members and empty variadic
arguments (`8461ee2`) the full ARM rerun gives **778 GCC cases and
1000 LLVM cases without vcc failures** (222 clang-filtered).  All four
caller combinations are run per case.  Together with the x86 run above,
the chosen set of 1000 generated cases per generator is now green on
both Linux targets among clang-valid cases.  ARM evidence:
`work/abi-generated/empty-fixed-arm/results.json`.  Extending seeds/counts
and later ABI changes require new runs.

**Combined rerun `5e17f47`:** after the later frame, atomic
and copy fixes, all clang-valid cases in all four caller combinations
pass again.  X86: **777 GCC + 1000 LLVM passed**, 222
filtered decimal cases and one clang crash (GCC 466).  ARM/QEMU: **778
GCC + 1000 LLVM passed**, 222 filtered decimal cases, no reference failures.
No vcc failures or vcc crashes on either target.  Reports:
`work/abi-generated/milestone-{x86,arm}/results.json`; both store the same
compiler hash as S3's compile milestone.  The kernel checks for the same
commit are also green: sema run/batch **887/887** each and PP parity
**920/920**.  The test harness for the compile milestone is separate in
[PR #27](https://github.com/jdahlin/vroom/pull/27); the upstream integration
of the compiler fixes is validated for a following PR.

**Done when:** N generated cases per platform without difference.

### S7 Random programs (TODO T10) — rig done, long runs remain
csmith and yarpgen are built from `work/testsuites/`.  `bench/vcc-csmith.py`
builds the programs with clang -O0 and vcc, runs them and compares checksums.
Deviations are reduced with creduce to tests in `test/vcc`.

Status 2026-11-11: both pinned generators built with CMake.
`LLVM=/usr/lib/llvm-23 bench/vcc-csmith.py --count 3 -j 2` saves sources,
seeds, commands, output and compiler hash in `work/random-tests/`.
Clang -O0, -O2 and UBSan with trap must give the same checksum before the
comparison; failures in the oracle are reported as skip, never as pass.  The
run uses its own copy of vpp so that concurrent rebuilds do not change the
compiler in the middle of a test.
YARPGen is limited in the smoke test to two array dimensions (`--max-array-dims=0`
restores the generator's default); the default seed 1 gave over 14 GB of
global arrays and clang could not link it with the small code model.
Seeds 1-3: x86 Csmith 3/3, YARPGen 2/3 (seed 3 hits X3's limit of
64 call arguments); ARM/QEMU Csmith 3/3, YARPGen 3/3.  The limits of
64 call arguments and 256 parameters have since been removed; a separate
rerun of seed 3 matches clang on both targets, and an ABI test with
291 parameters passes in all four compiler combinations.  No 24h run
or reduction is claimed to be done; S2-S6 must be verified first.

A bounded x86 Csmith run with four workers, `--duration 1800
--max-failures 100`, gave **1159 passed, 0 vcc failures and 200 filtered**.
151 were filtered at clang -O0's run (timeout), 49 at UBSan (trap).
Sources, commands and summary are in
`work/random-tests/20261111-083106-752654-x86_64-linux-gnu/`.
The rig saves intermediate results and stops scheduling when the time or
failure limit is reached; tests already running finish.  The failure limit
counts failing tests, not unique compiler bugs.  This does not replace the
24h gate.

A subsequent ARM/QEMU smoke test with Csmith seeds 3000000-3000019
(`52261ef`, two workers) gives **16 passed, 0 vcc failures and 4 filtered**
because of the clang -O0 run's timeout.  Evidence:
`work/random-tests/20261111-100225-3305520-aarch64-linux-gnu/`.
This is 20 generated tests, not a long run.

**Done when:** 24 h per generator without deviation, on both targets
(PLAN-release G1).

### S8 libc-test (TODO T13) — started
The first run of the 343 dynamic functional/math/regression tests
against glibc gives **303 clang-valid/vcc-passed on x86**, three reference
build failures and 37 reference run failures.  ARM's common test library was
blocked by `__builtin_isinf_sign` for long double.  The fix supports all FP
formats on ARM and also corrects x86's half/BF16 test and sign byte; both
build modes, five FP regressions per target and all 65536 half/BF16 patterns
pass.  With the isolated fix and vcc-built support objects, **309 ARM tests**
pass, three reference build failures and 31 reference run failures.  These
are targeted/isolated results.  The frozen PR combination `511d091` is then
rebuilt and fully rerun: **303/303 clang-valid x86 tests** followed by
**309/309 ARM/QEMU tests**, the same reference exceptions, zero candidate
failures or infrastructure failures and correct final status.  The
compiler's SHA256 is
`249d770ac3e0cb73c669637a9e20c284bc1018aee674cad547cc66c21f32cfef`;
full artifacts are in `/tmp/libc-pr-{x86,arm}`.  All 343 dynamic
configurations per target are accounted for, also those where the glibc
oracle fails.

`bench/vcc-libc-test.py` now follows upstream's Makefile/.mk graph for all
343 dynamic functional/math/regression binaries.  Each compiler builds
its own support objects and DSOs; clang assembles/links.  The rig keeps
flags, sources, commands, binaries, status and compiler hash in a new
artifact directory.  ARM uses QEMU and an explicit private libcrypt overlay
with verified glibc symbol requirements; `-lcrypt` is not removed.  The full
results above are verified; eleven checkout-only harness tests run in premerge.
Ordinary clang rejections/libc deviations are filtered separately, while
compiler crashes, timeouts, missing tools/rules and unexplained make failures
must give a failure status even if other tests pass.  An injected clang SIGSEGV
is verified red; a normal pow10 rejection together with valid tests
and the whole x86 run ends correctly.

The rig also maps the whole pinned graph with `--suite full --linkage both`:
**565 contracts** (345 dynamic targets, 141 static targets and 79 API objects).
The API's full link aggregates are kept unchanged; the generated API header
is preprocessed with the respective compiler.  `--linkage static` follows
upstream's dependencies on dynamic tests and reports their side results
separately.
Full separate verification against `7afcdea` gives **424 passing x86
contracts**, 83 reference build failures, 57 reference run failures and one
upstream-disabled test; ARM/QEMU gives **430 passed**, 83/51 reference
failures and the same disabled test.  Zero vcc failures and zero
infrastructure failures.  Eleven offline tests check graph, status,
preprocessing choice and that a disabled recipe actually matches
upstream.  Artifacts: `/tmp/libc-expanded-full-{x86,arm}` and
`/tmp/libc-expanded-evidence.json`.

The API's strict unchanged flags make Clang reject 79 of 80 contracts
in this glibc environment; these are not claimed to verify vcc.  Musl's
dynamic `pleval` is explicitly disabled by upstream, and the static test
needs a private libc function (Clang already rejects the source here).
A static ARM reference test gave SIGBUS in an earlier separate run
and passed in the full run; both artifacts are preserved.  Full
S8 conformance or musl validation is still not claimed.

musl's libc-test requires a real libc.  That is done when vcc builds musl, or
against glibc in the Linux container.  The functional tests and the math tests
(floating-point edge cases) are the interesting ones.

### S9 Commercial suite — decision
SuperTest (or Plum Hall) is needed only if 1.0 is to claim conformance
formally.  The decision is made when S2-S7 are green.

## Order
S2 (the crash, then the gaps by size) -> S3 -> S5 -> S4 -> S6 -> S7.  S3
and S5 are almost free with the existing rig.  S7 finds the most faulty
code per hour, but requires that S3-S5 first have taken the known failures.
